en the kinematic and inertial forces plays an important role in seismic of piles. Moreover, single and group piles respond differently to the seismic excitation. In this study, a centrifuge test program was extensively performed to address the kinematic–inertial interaction for single and group end-bearing piles. The interaction was investigated by means of analyzing the phase relation of displacement responses. Major conclusions can be drawn as follows.\n\nResults of centrifuge model tests demonstrated that the rocking motion significantly affected the seismic response of the pile group regarding the number of piles in the group in the shaking direction and rotational stiffness. For instance, the 2 × 2 pile group suffered considerably larger pile head displacement and bending moment in comparison with the 3 × 3 pile group.\n\nThe single pile displaced in the opposite direction with soil movement, whereas the group piles revealed a different behavior. The 3 × 3 group piles moved nearly in phase with the ground. However, the phase difference between the 2 × 2 group piles and the ground increased with the input acceleration amplitude because the effect of the rocking behavior became increasingly significant.\n\nThe presence of slope had a significant effect on the pile group compared with the single pile, which caused an asymmetry of bending moment distribution with the reference of the pile axis. For pile groups, the location of the maximum bending moment below the ground surface changed with the direction of shaking and pile position.\n\nThe kinematic force acted as resistance to the single pile throughout shaking. Conversely, group piles showed a large bending moment caused by the kinematic effect due to the large foundation stiffness of the pile group. The influence depth of the inertial force was approximately 10 times pile diameter below the ground surface for group piles and varied with the number of piles in the group and the amplitude of input motion. The dif